/// A numeric literal with a sign is a constant of the code, `-x` and `~x`
/// are one instruction each that the loop of instructions runs for a
/// Number, and `+x` of a Number stays in the loop; anything else is made a
/// numeric value the general way first, as before. Literals of every
/// spelling with either sign, the zeros, BigInt literals; a signed literal
/// in comparisons, a `switch`, default values and as an operand; each
/// operator, and each two of them, on values of every kind; objects that
/// convert by valueOf, toString and Symbol.toPrimitive, in the order they
/// are asked, those that throw and one that changes what it is read from;
/// operands that are locals, closure names, properties, getters, calls and
/// elements; a name before its declaration; values assigned back in a
/// loop; the operators as tests; the truncations; strict code; the value
/// of a statement. What is expected is what Node prints for the same
/// script.
#[test]
fn signed_literals_and_unary_operators_are_what_the_steps_give() {
    std::thread::Builder::new()
        .stack_size(8 << 20)
        .spawn(numbers_negated_and_complemented)
        .expect("thread")
        .join()
        .expect("checks");
}

